## Objective

Build a small production-style Flutter example that demonstrates:

1. A pure mixin
2. A constrained mixin
3. Multiple mixins
4. A `mixin class`
5. Composition
6. The architectural difference between mixin-based reuse and object-based collaboration

## Step 1 — Pure Mixin

```dart
mixin Logging {
  void log(String message) {
    print('[LOG] $message');
  }
}
```

Use it:

```dart
class AnalyticsService with Logging {
  void track(String event) {
    log('Tracking $event');
  }
}
```

## Step 2 — Constrained Mixin

```dart
class Controller {
  void notifyListeners() {
    print('Listeners notified');
  }
}

mixin LoadingState on Controller {
  bool isLoading = false;

  void startLoading() {
    isLoading = true;
    notifyListeners();
  }

  void stopLoading() {
    isLoading = false;
    notifyListeners();
  }
}
```

Use it:

```dart
class ProductController extends Controller with LoadingState {}
```

The mixin can safely use `notifyListeners()` because its `on Controller` constraint guarantees that capability.

## Step 3 — Multiple Mixins

```dart
mixin Logging {
  void log(String message) {
    print('[LOG] $message');
  }
}

mixin Metrics {
  void recordMetric(String name) {
    print('[METRIC] $name');
  }
}

class ProductService with Logging, Metrics {
  void loadProducts() {
    log('Loading products');
    recordMetric('products.load');
  }
}
```

The class receives both reusable capabilities.

## Step 4 — Mixin Class

```dart
mixin class Logger {
  void log(String message) {
    print(message);
  }
}
```

As a class:

```dart
final logger = Logger();
logger.log('Hello');
```

As a mixin:

```dart
class PaymentService with Logger {}
```

Use this dual capability intentionally rather than automatically.

## Step 5 — Composition Alternative

Instead of a logging mixin:

```dart
class Logger {
  void log(String message) {
    print('[LOG] $message');
  }
}

class PaymentService {
  final Logger logger;

  PaymentService(this.logger);

  void pay() {
    logger.log('Payment started');
  }
}
```

Now the logger is an explicit dependency.

## Step 6 — Test Substitution

Composition allows replacement:

```dart
abstract interface class Logger {
  void log(String message);
}

class ConsoleLogger implements Logger {
  @override
  void log(String message) {
    print(message);
  }
}

class FakeLogger implements Logger {
  final List<String> messages = [];

  @override
  void log(String message) {
    messages.add(message);
  }
}
```

Then:

```dart
class PaymentService {
  final Logger logger;

  PaymentService(this.logger);
}
```

This demonstrates why composition can be preferable when the behavior is an independently replaceable service.

## Step 7 — Flutter Integration

Create a small product screen with:

```text
ProductScreen
      ↓
ProductController
      ↓
Repository
```

The controller may use a constrained mixin for local loading-state behavior:

```text
ProductController
      ↓
LoadingState mixin
```

The repository should remain an explicit dependency rather than becoming a mixin merely to demonstrate code reuse.

## Step 8 — Mixin Method Resolution

Create two mixins that override the same method:

```dart
class BaseService {
  void execute() {
    print('Base');
  }
}

mixin First {
  void execute() {
    print('First');
    super.execute();
  }
}

mixin Second {
  void execute() {
    print('Second');
    super.execute();
  }
}

class Service extends BaseService with First, Second {}
```

Run the code and observe the execution order.

Then reverse the mixin order:

```dart
class Service extends BaseService with Second, First {}
```

Observe the difference.

This exercise is intended to make mixin application order concrete rather than theoretical.

## Step 9 — Production Decision

For each of the following, decide whether mixin or composition is more appropriate:

```text
Logging behavior
HTTP client
Payment gateway
Loading state helper
Analytics client
Retry policy
Database connection
Validation capability
```

For each decision, document:

- Why the behavior belongs to the consuming class or separate object
- Whether state is introduced
- Whether independent lifecycle exists
- Whether substitution/testing matters
- Whether the dependency should be explicit
